b/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093196 #edb/ref 1061 37594 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093196 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "7e843dd4-2f8a-5237-87b2-28b5a09adb3d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093196 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186092312 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093197 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186093199 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093197 #edb/ref 1061 37596 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093197 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "82947aff-7f67-51bc-9a88-6e5d204e1c08" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093197 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186093472 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093198 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186093231 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093198 #edb/ref 1061 37829 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093198 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2236e99f-a53e-57c2-91ab-c3091e8a86a9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093198 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186093473 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093199 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186093232 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093199 #edb/ref 1061 37830 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093199 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "6b4c7081-0037-5d22-8c22-bf18957c01d2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093199 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186093474 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093230 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186093198 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093230 #edb/ref 1061 4197 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093230 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "90c92812-0369-53f2-8139-3a602c9d8ddf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093230 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186093380 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093231 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186093195 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093231 #edb/ref 1061 4198 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093231 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "11713b46-54cf-58b5-8dc5-2984d413ed5f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093231 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186092327 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093232 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186093233 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093232 #edb/ref 1061 4201 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093232 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "bd94fe81-ec77-53b8-8ef8-e461ac344d67" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093232 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186092328 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093233 #edb/ref 1061 4204 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093233 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "cabcbccd-aabb-5b9e-a421-ce222693f535" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093233 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186092329 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093324 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186093325 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093324 #edb/ref 1061 657 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093324 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "4a6af753-c85c-59f9-be67-03ca672b3493" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093324 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186093430 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093325 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186093326 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093325 #edb/ref 1061 658 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093325 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "dabdab57-40b6-5940-8089-29b4010d85ea" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093325 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186092382 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093326 #edb/ref 1061 663 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093326 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "53c42827-7f73-5fef-997e-9f666fc35841" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093326 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186092383 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093380 #edb/ref 1064 "When we add, subtract, or multiply two integers, the answer is always an integer. \n\nTo illustrate, let's consider the following two integers:\n\n$$\na = 3, \\qquad b=2\n$$\n\n- When we *add* the two integers, we get an integer:\n\n$$\na + b = 3+2 = 5\\quad{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}\n$$\n\n- When we *subtract* the two integers, we get an integer:\n\n$$\na - b = 3-2 = 1\\quad{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}\n$$\n\n- When we *multiply* the two integers, we get an integer:\n\n$$\na \\cdot b = 3\\cdot 2 = 6\\quad{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}\n$$\n\n- However, dividing the two integers does *not* give an integer:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{a}{b} = \\dfrac32 \\quad{\\color{red}{\\boldsymbol\\times}}\n$$\n\nWe say the integers are **closed** under addition, subtraction, and multiplication but **not closed** under division.\n\n**Watch Out!** Although integers are not closed under division, the ratio of two integers is *sometimes* an integer. For example, if $a=4$ and $b=2,$ then\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{a}{b} =  \\dfrac{4}{2} = 2,\n$$\n\nwhich is an integer." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093380 #edb/ref 1065 1593 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093380 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "a7d0a253-b58a-5205-b05d-d5d2f1d49f6e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093380 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093430 #edb/ref 1064 "The equation of the tangent line to the curve $y = f(x)$ at the point $P(x_1,y_1),$ in point-slope form, is given by\n\n$$\ny - y_1 = m (x - x_1), \\hspace{.75cm} m=f'(x_1).\n$$\n\nHere, $m = f'(x_1)$ because $f'(x_1)$ represents the slope of the tangent line to $y=f(x)$ at the point $(x_1,y_1).$\n\nFor example, suppose we want to compute the equation of the tangent line to the curve $y=x^2$ at the point $P(1,1).$\n\n![](images/48/487d21289b468fb82efa2097e425dd560811dc3165890509dacbab8093e0ecdb.png)\n\nSince $y=x^2,$ we have $f(x)=x^2.$ We begin by calculating the derivative of $f(x)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\nf'(x) \t\t = \\dfrac {\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}(x^2)= 2x\n$$\n\nThen, we determine the slope of the tangent at $P(1,1)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\nm = f'(1)  = 2(1) = 2\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we substitute the slope $m=2$ and the coordinates of $P(1,1)$ into the point-slope form, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-1 & =2(x-1) \\\\ y & =2x-2+1 \\\\ y & =2x-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the tangent is $y=2x-1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093430 #edb/ref 1065 354 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093430 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "8b0ed179-00e3-5e1a-84fc-c78773cfef19" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093430 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093471 #edb/ref 1064 "Consider the following polynomials:\n\n$$\nP(x) = 2x^{\\color{blue}4} - 3x + 1, \\qquad\nQ(x) = -6x^{\\color{red}3} + 5x^2 + x\n$$\n\nNotice that these polynomials have degrees $4$ and $3,$ respectively. We write this as\n\n$$\n\\textrm{deg}(P) = {\\color{blue}4}, \\qquad\n\\textrm{deg}(Q) = {\\color{red}3}.\n$$\n\nFurthermore, \n\n- the leading term in $P(x)$ is $2x^{\\color{blue}4},$ and\n\n- the leading term in $Q(x)$ is $-6x^{\\color{red}3}$\n\nAs a result, the leading term in the product $P \\cdot Q$ equals the product of the leading terms of $P$ and $Q\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n2x^{\\color{blue}4} \\cdot (-6x^{\\color{red}3}) = -12x^{{\\color{blue}4}+{\\color{red}3}} = -12x^{\\color{purple}7}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the degree of $P \\cdot Q$ must be ${\\color{purple}{7}}.$\n\nWe have the following general rule:\n\n*The degree of the product of two polynomials equals the sum of the degrees of these polynomials:* \n\n$$\n\\textrm{deg}(P \\cdot Q) = \\textrm{deg}(P) + \\textrm{deg}(Q)\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093471 #edb/ref 1065 6201 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093471 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "60187490-7cd6-566d-ab45-128e2c4f81c3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093471 #edb/ref 1067 "The Degree of a Product of Two Polynomials" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093472 #edb/ref 1064 "Let's now consider the degree of the sum and difference of two polynomials $P$ and $Q.$ \n\nFirst, let's suppose that $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ have *different* degrees. In this case, the degree of the sum or difference equals the largest of the two degrees. We write\n\n$$\n\\textrm{deg}(P \\pm Q) = \\max \\big\\{ \\textrm{deg}(P), \\textrm{deg}(Q) \\big\\},\n$$\n\n \nwhere the $\\textrm{max}$ function simply returns the largest element of the set.\n\nFor example, suppose that $\\textrm{deg}(P) = 5$ and $\\textrm{deg}(Q) = 2.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\textrm{deg}(P + Q) = \\max \\big\\{ 5, 2 \\big\\} = 5.\n$$\n\n \n\nTo see why this is true, note that the leading term of $P$ contains an $x^5,$ and the leading term of $Q$ has an $x^2.$ When we add the two polynomials, no term of $Q$ could cancel out the leading term of $P.$ So the sum of the polynomials must have degree $5.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093472 #edb/ref 1065 6202 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093472 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "fae91d47-b786-5c2f-9a29-dfb06624930f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093472 #edb/ref 1067 "Degrees of Sums and Differences of Polynomials: Case 1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093473 #edb/ref 1064 "Polynomials are also closed under addition, subtraction, and multiplication but not closed under division.\n\nTo illustrate, let's consider the following two polynomials:\n\n$$\nP(x) = x+1, \\qquad Q(x) = x^2\n$$\n\n- When we *add* the two polynomials, we get a polynomial:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P+Q & =(x+1)+({x}^{2}) \\\\  & =x+1+{x}^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+x+1\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- When we *subtract* the two polynomials, we get a polynomial:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P-Q & =(x+1)-({x}^{2}) \\\\  & =x+1-{x}^{2} \\\\  & =-{x}^{2}+x+1\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- When we *multiply* the two polynomials, we get a polynomial:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P\\cdot Q & =(x+1)\\cdot ({x}^{2}) \\\\  & =x\\cdot {x}^{2}+1\\cdot {x}^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{3}+{x}^{2}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- However, *dividing* the two polynomials does *not* give a polynomial:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{P}{Q} & =\\frac{x+1}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Watch Out!** Although polynomials are not closed under division, the ratio of two polynomials is *sometimes* a polynomial. For example, if $P=x^2$ and $Q=x,$ then\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{P}{Q} =  \\dfrac{x^2}{x} = x,\n$$\n\nwhich is a polynomial." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093473 #edb/ref 1065 6228 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093473 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "d529e51a-742c-52f5-874b-546cf98a1849" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093473 #edb/ref 1067 "Closure Properties of Polynomials" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093474 #edb/ref 1064 "Now suppose that $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ have the *same* degree. That is\n\n$$\n\\textrm{deg}(P) =   \\textrm{deg}(Q) = n.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn this case, the degree of the sum or difference can be *any integer* from $0$ to $n.$\n\n$$\n0 \\leq \\textrm{deg}(P \\pm Q) \\leq n\n$$\n\n \n\nLet's consider some examples:\n\n- Let $P$ and $Q$ be polynomials of degree $15.$ Then, the *largest possible degree* of $P+Q$ is $15$ since the resulting polynomial can't contain terms of order higher than $15.$\n\n- On the other hand, the *smallest possible degree* of $P+Q$ is $0.$ For instance, suppose $P=x^{15}$ and $Q=1-x^{15}.$ Then $P +Q = 1,$ which is a polynomial of degree $0.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093474 #edb/ref 1065 6229 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093474 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "f9d9fe12-e873-5b5b-aca8-f212f9be62b4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093474 #edb/ref 1067 "Degrees of Sums and Differences of Polynomials: Case 2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096447 #edb/ref 1069 "e-4282" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096447 #edb/ref 1071 "The vector $\\mathbf{a}$ has magnitude $3$. Find a unit vector that is parallel to $\\mathbf{a}$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096447 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2a61b115-4e26-5966-b2a9-d2fedef6b728" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096447 #edb/ref 1074 "To obtain the unit vector that is parallel to $\\mathbf{a}$, we divide $\\mathbf{a}$ by its length:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u}=\\dfrac{\\mathbf{a}}{\\mid \\,\\mathbf{a}\\, \\mid}=\\dfrac{1}{3}\\mathbf{a}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096448 #edb/ref 1069 "e-4283" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096448 #edb/ref 1071 "The vector $\\mathbf{a}$ is directed horizontally to the right and has magnitude $10$, while the vector $\\mathbf{b}$ is directed vertically down and has magnitude $20$. Find a unit vector that is parallel to $\\mathbf{a+b}$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096448 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "23d9a601-974b-51c2-a6ea-0ebadde19efc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096448 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we have to find $\\mathbf{a+b}$. According to the triangle law of addition, the resultant $\\mathbf{a}+\\mathbf{b}$ goes from the tail of $\\mathbf{a}$ to the head of $\\mathbf{b}$:\n\n![](images/32/32c48d8c19fc68fbc172b240ded922f3e2ebc12804411c318ff63fe999aaedcc.png)\n\nTo find the unit vector corresponding to $\\mathbf{a+b}$, we have to divide by the magnitude:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu &= \\frac{a + b}{\\mid a + b \\mid}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the magnitude, first notice that we obtain a right triangle. So, we can use the Pythagorean theorem:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{vmatrix}a + b & ^{2} &= & a & ^{2} +\\left.\\end{vmatrix}b\\right|^{2} \\\\\n&= 10^{2} + 20^{2} \\\\\n&= 100 + 400 \\\\\n&= 500\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the magnitude is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mid \\mathbf{a+b} &= \\sqrt{500}=10\\sqrt{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the corresponding unit vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu &= \\frac{a + b}{\\mid a + b \\mid} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{10\\sqrt{5}}(a + b)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096493 #edb/ref 1069 "e-5181" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096493 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/d3/d3042242136b82b867b8bd7911761ee38318924df0c9979488d20f04117c45d9.png)\n\nConsider the picture shown above. Find a unit vector that is parallel to $\\mathbf a$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096493 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6dfe09f0-30e2-53bc-a8f6-d7e4a3b8232a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096493 #edb/ref 1074 "![](images/b3/b316448c885a20a7ed60871ce0f1a4dc4f77bd4658dc6eda3407fb3c89a088cb.png)\n\nIn $\\bigtriangleup AHB$ we have\n\n$$\n\\angle AHB = 90^\\circ\n$$\n\n, $AH=3$ and $BH=2$. So, the Pythagorean theorem gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nAB &= \\sqrt{AH^{2} + BH^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{3^{2} + 2^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{9 + 4} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{13}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the magnitude of $\\mathbf{a}$ is $\\sqrt{13}$. Consequently, the unit vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu &= \\frac{1}{\\mid a \\mid} \\cdot a \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}a\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096813 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048882 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096813 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049715 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096813 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186096969 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096813 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186097045 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096813 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186502222 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186096813 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186502223 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186096813 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186502225 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186096813 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186502232 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186096813 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186502236 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186096813 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186502240 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186096813 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186502241 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186096813 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186502242 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186096813 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186502251 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186096813 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186502260 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186096813 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186502272 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186096813 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186502278 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186096813 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186502283 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186096813 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186502297 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186096813 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186502319 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186096813 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding a Unit Vector Parallel to a Given Vector" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096813 #edb/ref 1040 #edb/ref 17592186502322 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186096813 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186096447 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096813 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "48a66a99-6b4a-5ad5-8922-6f48006ff183" #edb/