7592186096884 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096884 #edb/ref 1045 "Unit Vectors" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096884 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186097737 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096884 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049360 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096884 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "886871c8-0b6e-56ce-bc7f-54affec4fd88" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097737 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186097738 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097737 #edb/ref 1061 430121 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097737 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "961ff0d0-9cef-5a2d-8207-63481c0cc1ab" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097737 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186097833 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097738 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186097739 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097738 #edb/ref 1061 430122 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097738 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "e301e3f9-501f-5f63-afac-f53379b62c13" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097738 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186096813 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097739 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186097740 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097739 #edb/ref 1061 430125 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097739 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "957d3735-2041-5bec-9e7c-4c1623dd4899" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097739 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186096814 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097740 #edb/ref 1061 430128 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097740 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "11ad8934-d3f9-546c-b161-9058d251ac66" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097740 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186096815 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097833 #edb/ref 1064 "A **unit vector** is a vector whose magnitude is equal to $1$.\n\nGiven any vector $\\mathbf a$, we can create a unit vector $\\mathbf u$ by dividing $\\mathbf a$ by its magnitude $\\mid \\, \\mathbf{a} \\, \\mid$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u} = \\dfrac{\\mathbf{a}}{\\mid \\,\\mathbf{a}\\, \\mid}\n$$\n\nFor example, if\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mid \\,\\mathbf{a}\\, &= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthen the unit vector is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u} = \\dfrac{\\mathbf{a}}{2} = \\dfrac{1}{2} \\mathbf{a}\n$$\n\n![](images/c7/c78d5b75c1d5928e7b269a4a67229487b3d3324e9815b11c5fd1f47f63437d82.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097833 #edb/ref 1065 2906 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097833 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "a90fd4b3-d41f-589f-9067-39d57e3e8712" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097833 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100620 #edb/ref 1069 "e-28" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100620 #edb/ref 1071 "Calculate $\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow 2} x^{-5}$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100620 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "04538f07-6e4c-547e-be0c-8b62025e19ff" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100620 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we will express the negative exponent as a quotient:\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow 2} x^{-5}= \\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow 2} \\dfrac{1}{x^5}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the quotient rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → 2)x^{-5} &= (lim_(x → 2)1)/(lim_(x → 2)x^{5}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2^{5}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{32}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100621 #edb/ref 1069 "e-29" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100621 #edb/ref 1071 "Find $\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow 2} \\dfrac{xf(x)+x^2}{2+f(x)}$ for the function $f(x)$ plotted below.\n\n![](images/5b/5b29133a4e82b885aa3e20e423719564a61bfff5b33472addf5036d3a18efd3c.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100621 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "71b3f83c-33e6-5043-9215-7db7b4b12497" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100621 #edb/ref 1074 "Using the quotient rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → 2)(xf(x) + x^{2})/(2 + f(x)) &= (lim_(x → 2)(xf(x) + x^{2}))/(lim_(x → 2)(2 + f(x)))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe find from the graph that\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow 2} f(x)=3\n$$\n\nCalculating the limits of the numerator and denominator separately, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → 2)(xf(x) + x^{2}) &= 2(3) + 2^{2} = 10, \\\\\nlim_(x → 2)(2 + f(x)) &= 2 + 3 = 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTaking the quotient of these limits, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → 2)(xf(x) + x^{2})/(2 + f(x)) &= (lim_(x → 2)(xf(x) + x^{2}))/(lim_(x → 2)(2 + f(x))) \\\\\n&= \\frac{10}{5} \\\\\n&= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100658 #edb/ref 1069 "e-3561" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100658 #edb/ref 1071 "Find $\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow 2}\\left(\\dfrac{x^3 f(x)}{4}\\right)$ for the function $f(x)$ plotted below.\n\n![](images/f4/f41914a12cf2ce0ec4799c4e9bacc3347e133f99747512a2211428e3571c4677.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100658 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0f441b48-b977-538c-82af-aa40ed4076d2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100658 #edb/ref 1074 "From the graph, we get that\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow 2} f(x)=4\n$$\n\nSo, applying the product rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → 2)((x^{3}f(x))/(4)) &= \\frac{1}{4} \\cdot lim_(x → 2)x^{3} \\cdot lim_(x → 2)f(x) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{4} \\cdot 2^{3} \\cdot 4 \\\\\n&= 8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100659 #edb/ref 1069 "e-3562" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100659 #edb/ref 1071 "Find $\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow -2}(x^2-3x)(x+1)^{3}$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100659 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7a24a8e0-768e-541c-aff1-cad6f977da87" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100659 #edb/ref 1074 "Applying the product rule, followed by the sum and constant multiple rules, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → - 2)(x^{2} - 3x)(x + 1)^{3} &= lim_(x → - 2)(x^{2} - 3x) \\cdot lim_(x → - 2)(x + 1)^{3} \\\\\n&= (lim_(x → - 2)x^{2} - 3lim_(x → - 2)x)(lim_(x → - 2)(x^{3} + 3x^{2} + 3x + 1)) \\\\\n&= ((-2)^{2} - 3(-2))((-2)^{3} + 3(-2)^{2} + 3(-2) + 1) \\\\\n&= (4 + 6)(-8 + 12 - 6 + 1) \\\\\n&= (10)(-1) \\\\\n&=-10\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100823 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186101387 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100823 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186101388 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100823 #edb/ref 1039 "Applying the Product Rule to Compute a Limit Given a Graph" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100823 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186100658 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100823 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "23567a86-8e1e-5b94-bac6-09c79cdb42c9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100824 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050653 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100824 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186101385 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100824 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186101386 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100824 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186233420 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100824 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186235427 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100824 #edb/ref 1039 "Applying the Product Rule to Compute a Limit" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100824 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186100659 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100824 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "5feb914d-efad-5d89-85a9-db99befc9928" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100952 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186101389 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100952 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186101419 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100952 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186235468 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100952 #edb/ref 1039 "Applying the Quotient Rule to Compute a Limit" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100952 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186100620 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100952 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "0a649e49-73c7-5470-8361-2002da74f234" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100953 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186101241 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100953 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186101242 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100953 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186235507 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100953 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186512873 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100953 #edb/ref 1039 "Applying the Quotient Rule to Compute a Limit Given a Graph" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100953 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186100621 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100953 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "b0159149-010c-5bef-a28d-85c4c43f0586" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101087 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186101602 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101087 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186101603 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101087 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186101796 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101087 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186101797 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101087 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186101798 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101087 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186101799 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101087 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101087 #edb/ref 1045 "The Product and Quotient Rules for Limits" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101087 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186101796 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101087 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049226 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101087 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "af8776aa-b3ed-5305-95b5-bf42623f4ed3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101602 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186101797 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101602 #edb/ref 1061 12001 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101602 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2408ea61-ca9b-52c5-ae47-17af57a34e64" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101602 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186100823 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101603 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186101602 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101603 #edb/ref 1061 12002 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101603 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a3d559f2-c362-56ee-804c-080f88071508" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101603 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186100824 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101796 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186101603 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101796 #edb/ref 1061 333 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101796 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "55b6421a-40da-531f-9d53-41329827a526" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101796 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186101928 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101797 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186101798 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101797 #edb/ref 1061 339 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101797 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "67532783-b8e9-5de2-9ca2-19a49fbf6ec4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101797 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186101929 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101798 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186101799 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101798 #edb/ref 1061 340 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101798 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "e8f2b1af-f0df-5d06-b349-280fb90373de" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101798 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186100952 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101799 #edb/ref 1061 342 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101799 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1da33225-c9cd-5fd9-8576-b2d8fdce7dcf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101799 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186100953 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101928 #edb/ref 1064 "The **product rule** states that the limit of the product of two functions equals the product of the limits.\n\nMore precisely, if\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\, a}f(x)=L\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\, a}g(x)=K\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → a)(f(x) \\cdot g(x)) &= lim_(x → a)f(x) \\cdot lim_(x → a)g(x) \\\\\n&= L \\cdot K\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor example, we can evaluate\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x \\rightarrow \\,2} \\left[ (x^2 +2x +3)(x^3-2x-1) \\right]\n$$\n\nby evaluating the limit of each polynomial separately, and then multiplying the results together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → 2)[(x^{2} + 2x + 3)(x^{3} - 2x - 1)] &= lim_(x → 2)(x^{2} + 2x + 3) \\cdot lim_(x → 2)(x^{3} - 2x - 1) \\\\\n&= (2^{2} + 2 \\cdot 2 + 3) \\cdot (2^{3} - 2 \\cdot 2 - 1) \\\\\n&= (4 + 4 + 3) \\cdot (8 - 4 - 1) \\\\\n&= (11) \\cdot (3) \\\\\n&= 33\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101928 #edb/ref 1065 175 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101928 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "c7f68019-2904-5740-87e6-e66d189cb771" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101928 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101929 #edb/ref 1064 "The **quotient rule** states that the limit of the quotient of two functions equals the quotient of the limits (provided that the limit of the denominator is not zero).\n\nMore precisely, if\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow a}f(x)=L\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow a}g(x)= K \\neq 0\n$$\n\n, then\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow a} \\dfrac{f(x)}{g(x)} = \\dfrac{\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow a}f(x)}{\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow a}g(x)}=\\dfrac{L}{K}\n$$\n\nFor example, we can evaluate\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x \\rightarrow \\,2} \\dfrac{x^2+4x}{x+1}\n$$\n\nby evaluating the limits of the numerator and denominator separately, and then dividing the results:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → 2)\\frac{x^{2} + 4x}{x + 1} &= (lim_(x → 2)(x^{2} + 4x))/(lim_(x → 2)(x + 1)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{2^{2} + 4 \\cdot 2}{2 + 1} \\\\\n&= \\frac{12}{3} \\\\\n&= 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101929 #edb/ref 1065 178 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101929 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "46d526ad-25bf-58f5-88e0-695b4ff6d430" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101929 #edb/ref 1067 "The Quotient Rule for Limits" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105560 #edb/ref 1069 "e-3289" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105560 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the area of $\\triangle{ABC}$ below. Round your answer to two decimal places.\n\n![](images/dc/dc625408598aa3cbfda44ec877c9a26ab563dee9fcf757192fcf2cf8411f606a.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105560 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "62405619-31ca-5403-aa45-360e6b3fbd59" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105560 #edb/ref 1074 "The formula for the area of the triangle is \n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A}=\\dfrac{1}{2}ab\\sin{C},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\angle{C}$ is between the sides of lengths $a$ and $b.$ \n\nHere, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =CB=58 \\\\ b & =CA=40 \\\\ m∠C & ={31}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince \n\n$$\n\\sin{31^{\\circ}} = 0.515\\,038\n$$\n\nrounded to six decimal places,  we calculate the area as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 58\\cdot 40\\operatorname{sin}⁡{31}^{∘} \\\\  & =1160\\cdot (0.515\\,038) \\\\  & ≈597.44,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105561 #edb/ref 1069 "e-3294" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105561 #edb/ref 1071 "Rounded to the nearest integer, what is the area of the quadrilateral $ABCD?$\n\n![](images/bc/bc9f32a9c0e8b042498a72e808a29274bd505e786d8ec43d0e0bc9888fdf6e44.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105561 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b41b2354-c339-5398-ab43-5886b8447c1c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105561 #edb/ref 1074 "The area of the quadrilateral $ABCD$ is the sum of the areas of the triangles $\\triangle ABC$ and $\\triangle ADC$ (see the illustration below).\n\n![](images/ac/acd2e923a65d0821cd59ed8b8274ceb5fc38def896467e37c8b1d454f958699e.png)\n\n First, we find the area for $\\triangle ABC,$ and get\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{ABC} & =\\frac{1}{2}(42)(35)\\operatorname{sin}⁡{36}^{∘} \\\\  & =735\\operatorname{sin}⁡{36}^{∘} \\\\  & ≈432.022\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places.\n\nNow we find the area for $\\triangle ADC,$ and get\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{ADC} & =\\frac{1}{2}(20)(18)\\operatorname{sin}⁡{80}^{∘} \\\\  & =180\\operatorname{sin}⁡{80}^{∘} \\\\  & ≈177.265\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places.\n\nFinally, we add together the areas of our two triangles to determine the area of $ABCD.$ We get\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{ABCD} & ={A}_{ABC}+{A}_{ADC} \\\\  & =432.022+177.265 \\\\  & ≈609\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, to the nearest integer, the area of the quadrilateral $ABCD$ is $609.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105562 #edb/ref 1069 "e-3297" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105562 #edb/ref 1071 "The area of $ABCD$ is $300\\, \\text{cm}^2.$  What is the area of $\\triangle ABC$ rounded to the nearest integer?\n\n![](images/c0/c06ffb123bdd24c3f83419767b6d1578223b8093089b79f91aae8f1fc6121305.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105562 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "472216c8-da7d-58fb-9056-e71d5e33460d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105562 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's start by finding the area of $\\triangle ACD.$ We get \n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{ACD} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot DA\\cdot DC\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡D \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(25)(32)\\operatorname{sin}⁡{70}^{∘} \\\\  & =400\\operatorname{sin}⁡{70}^{∘} \\\\  & =375.877... \\\\  & ≈376\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer.\n\nThe difference of the areas of the quadrilateral $ABCD$ and $\\triangle ACD$ gives the area of $\\triangle ABC.$ Therefore, \n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{ABC} & ={A}_{ACD}-{A}_{ABCD} \\\\  & =376-300 \\\\  & =76\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105579 #edb/ref 1069 "e-4253" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105579 #edb/ref 1071 "The area of ​​the triangle $\\triangle ABC$ is $24\\sqrt{2} \\text{ cm}^2.$ Find $AB.$\n\n![](images/e9/e93e732bd04cfda304c2098087548a638d8d869ccf8b1050235e8207825875d3.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105579 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c8d31ea4-41c6-5f24-bf7a-a950a30342d7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105579 #edb/ref 1074 "The formula for the area of the triangle is \n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A}=\\dfrac{1}{2}bc\\sin{A},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\angle{A}$ is between the sides of lengths $b$ and $c.$ \n\nHere\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =AB, \\\\ b & =AC=12\\text{ cm}, \\\\ m∠A & ={45}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince \n\n$$\n\\sin{45^{\\circ}} = \\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\n$$\n\nwe can calculate $c=AB,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot b\\cdot c\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡A \\\\ 24\\sqrt{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 12\\cdot c\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡{45}^{∘} \\\\ 24\\sqrt{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 12\\cdot c\\cdot (\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\ 24\\cancel{\\sqrt{2}} & =3\\cancel{\\sqrt{2}}c \\\\ 24 & =3c \\\\ c & =8\\text{ cm}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that $AB = 8\\,\\textrm{cm}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105903 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049449 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105903 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186106085 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105903 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186106086 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105903 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186284783 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105903 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186295109 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105903 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186295110 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105903 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186295123 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105903 #edb/ref 1039 "Calculating the Area of a Triangle" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105903 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186105560 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105903 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "acc86fb6-2ef4-5f8f-b204-6e1fdbbf0530" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105904 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050847 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105904 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186106346 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105904 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186106347 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105904 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186278381 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105904 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186278383 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105904 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186295125 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105904 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186295126 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105904 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186323821 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105904 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186329720 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105904 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186329721 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105904 #edb/ref 1039 "Calculating an Angle or Side of a Triangle Given the Area of a Triangle" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105904 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186105579 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105904 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "cafdb077-0e02-5e7a-bf78-c3a8